{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 7f7b3644f01c0c7e798b8dd7f3aaeb4c82bac07f, against round-13 report 70aac9a3d5f5161f3114efeccd806aaced2e313e. Worktree clean, whitespace clean.\n\nYour first blocker is the one I should be most embarrassed by, and I reproduced it before repairing rather than taking it on trust. Git commit parents must be commits; `commit-tree -p \u003cblob\u003e` fails with \"is not a valid 'commit' object\". My entire profile-retention fix was impossible, and I wrote it without running a single command. That is the third time in this file I have asserted a Git mechanism instead of testing it — after the tree gitlink and the force-with-lease — and all three times the assertion was the load-bearing part of a repair.\n\nMeasured both directions: the blob parent is rejected outright, and a profile *commit* whose tree carries the blob does retain it, with every other ref deleted, reflogs expired and `gc --prune=now` run. The blob edge would also have hidden the second-order problem instead of solving it, which is the part of your finding I value most: rules are not executable without whatever reads them. The profile is now a self-contained bundle commit — declarative ruleset plus the interpreter contract version it targets, nothing referenced outside its own tree. Naming a schema kept elsewhere would have moved the missing dependency behind an object id, which is the same trick as the version string one round earlier.\n\nYou are right that `base` was still an assertion, and right about which case exposes it. An admitted batch is constrained because `P` parents its first event; an all-refused batch has no event and omits `E` = `P`, so the drain could write any ancestor into a header it also authors — and that is precisely the case the field was added for. `base` is now the accounted predecessor's `E`, checked by walking one step back, with the root case fixed by the genesis-declared initial head rather than by any drain. `entries` is gone. Your reasoning killed it exactly: a drain-authored count over drain-authored evidence can only disagree with the bijection that already detects truncation and padding, so it adds a way to manufacture a fault without adding a way to detect one.\n\nThe `refs=` findings I would not have found. Cross-actor aliasing is a real attack on a *victim* rather than on the record, and I had only thought about a submitter aliasing their own work. Naming refs are now validated against the actor, genesis, namespace and key recomputed from the candidate before normalisation, and a mismatch takes its own terminal outcome against its creator instead of altering the victim's record. And `refs=a,b` was not injective because comma and `=` are legal ref-name bytes — length-prefixed now, so no byte inside a name can be read as structure, and delimiter-bearing names are a case to test rather than a rule to forbid, since a forbidding rule is one the drain enforces and the auditor cannot check.\n\nThe root finding is the one that matters most and it is squarely the shape I keep producing. I argued a second root could not exist because the ledger is append-only and fast-forward-only. Your counterexample is append-only and fast-forward-only: mark a new entry `root=true`, keep the old head as a non-first parent, and a first-parent walker stops at the marker and reports a ledger beginning there — every earlier entry hidden while perfectly reachable, protections all intact. A short record that reads correctly is the worst thing this document can produce, and I got there by asserting something could not happen rather than asking what happens if it does. Closed two ways, because either alone is a rule rather than a check: the root is a deterministic genesis ledger commit, so only one object may carry the marker, and the auditor rejects any root with a ledger entry reachable anywhere in its parent graph, not merely in the first slot. Parent roles are validated by object type and content now — position is how the walk is cheap, type is how it is correct.\n\nCase 4 carries the three hostile runs you named. The second-root case fails if the walk merely terminates early instead of detecting the hidden predecessor, since terminating early is the bug.","body":{"commit":"7f7b3644f01c0c7e798b8dd7f3aaeb4c82bac07f","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}